# Next.js + TypeScript + React Development Rules

## Code Style and Structure

### TypeScript
- Use TypeScript for all files (.ts, .tsx)
- Prefer interfaces over types for object shapes
- Use type for unions, intersections, and primitives
- Enable strict mode and fix all type errors
- Avoid `any` - use `unknown` if type is truly unknown
- Use proper return types for functions
- Leverage type inference where it improves readability

### React Components
- Use functional components with hooks
- Prefer named exports for components
- Use proper TypeScript types for props (interface ComponentProps)
- Destructure props in function parameters
- Use React.FC sparingly (prefer explicit prop types)
- Keep components focused and single-responsibility
- Extract complex logic into custom hooks

### File Organization
- Group related files in feature folders
- Use index.ts for clean exports
- Keep components, hooks, utils, and types separate
- Follow Next.js App Router conventions:
  - page.tsx for pages
  - layout.tsx for layouts
  - loading.tsx for loading states
  - error.tsx for error boundaries
  - route.ts for API routes

### Naming Conventions
- Components: PascalCase (UserProfile.tsx)
- Files: kebab-case for non-components (user-utils.ts)
- Hooks: camelCase starting with 'use' (useUserData)
- Constants: UPPER_SNAKE_CASE
- Types/Interfaces: PascalCase (UserProfile, ApiResponse)
- Functions: camelCase (fetchUserData)

## Next.js Specific

### App Router
- Use server components by default
- Add 'use client' only when needed (hooks, browser APIs, event handlers)
- Leverage React Server Components for data fetching
- Use route groups (folders) for organization without affecting URL
- Implement proper error boundaries and loading states

### Performance
- Use next/image for all images
- Implement dynamic imports for code splitting
- Use metadata API for SEO
- Leverage caching strategies (fetch with cache options)
- Avoid client-side data fetching when server-side is possible

### Data Fetching
- Use async/await in server components
- Implement proper error handling with try/catch
- Use React Query for client-side data fetching
- Cache API responses appropriately
- Handle loading and error states

## Styling (Tailwind CSS + shadcn/ui)

### Tailwind
- Use Tailwind utility classes for styling
- Leverage the cn() utility for conditional classes
- Follow mobile-first responsive design
- Use theme colors from tailwind.config
- Avoid arbitrary values unless necessary

### shadcn/ui
- Import components from @/components/ui
- Customize components via className prop
- Use built-in variants when available
- Maintain consistent spacing and sizing
- Leverage Radix UI primitives for accessibility

## State Management

### Local State
- Use useState for simple component state
- Use useReducer for complex state logic
- Lift state up when shared between components

### Global State (Zustand)
- Create focused stores (not one giant store)
- Use selectors to prevent unnecessary rerenders
- Keep actions colocated with state
- Implement proper TypeScript types for stores

### Form State
- Use react-hook-form for forms
- Integrate with Zod for validation
- Implement proper error messaging
- Handle loading states during submission

## Database (Supabase)

### Queries
- Use TypeScript types generated from database
- Implement proper error handling
- Use select() to limit returned fields
- Leverage RLS (Row Level Security) policies
- Cache queries when appropriate

### Real-time
- Clean up subscriptions in useEffect cleanup
- Handle connection errors gracefully
- Implement optimistic updates for better UX

## Code Quality

### Best Practices
- Write self-documenting code
- Add comments for complex logic only
- Keep functions small and focused
- Follow DRY principle (Don't Repeat Yourself)
- Implement proper error boundaries
- Use early returns to reduce nesting

### Error Handling
- Always handle errors explicitly
- Provide user-friendly error messages
- Log errors for debugging (server-side)
- Implement proper fallback UI
- Use error boundaries for component errors

### Testing Mindset
- Write testable code (pure functions when possible)
- Avoid tight coupling
- Use dependency injection for external dependencies
- Keep side effects isolated

## Security

### Best Practices
- Never expose API keys in client code
- Use environment variables for secrets
- Validate all user inputs
- Implement proper authentication checks
- Use Supabase RLS for data access control
- Sanitize data before rendering (XSS prevention)

## Accessibility

### Standards
- Use semantic HTML elements
- Provide proper ARIA labels when needed
- Ensure keyboard navigation works
- Maintain proper heading hierarchy
- Use alt text for images
- Ensure sufficient color contrast
- shadcn/ui components are accessible by default (Radix UI)

## Performance Optimization

### React
- Memoize expensive calculations with useMemo
- Memoize callbacks with useCallback
- Use React.memo for expensive components
- Avoid inline object/array creation in props
- Implement proper key props for lists

### Next.js
- Use proper caching strategies
- Implement incremental static regeneration when appropriate
- Optimize images with next/image
- Use dynamic imports for code splitting
- Leverage edge functions for performance

## Import Organization

### Order
1. React and Next.js imports
2. Third-party libraries
3. Internal components
4. Internal utilities and hooks
5. Types and interfaces
6. Styles

### Example
```typescript
import { useState, useEffect } from 'react'
import { useRouter } from 'next/navigation'

import { Button } from '@/components/ui/button'
import { createClient } from '@supabase/supabase-js'

import { UserProfile } from '@/components/UserProfile'
import { useUserData } from '@/hooks/useUserData'
import { cn } from '@/lib/utils'

import type { User, Profile } from '@/types'
```

## Comments and Documentation

### When to Comment
- Complex algorithms or business logic
- Non-obvious workarounds or hacks
- Public API functions (JSDoc)
- Regular expressions
- Configuration objects with many options

### When NOT to Comment
- Self-explanatory code
- Variable declarations
- Simple functions
- Obvious operations

## Git Commit Messages

### Format
- Use imperative mood ("Add feature" not "Added feature")
- First line: brief summary (50 chars or less)
- Separate subject from body with blank line
- Body: explain what and why, not how
- Reference issues/PRs when applicable

### Examples
- "Add CSV export for registrations"
- "Fix roommate matching algorithm edge case"
- "Refactor user profile component for performance"
- "Update admin dashboard layout"

## Environment Variables

### Naming
- Prefix public vars with NEXT_PUBLIC_
- Use UPPER_SNAKE_CASE
- Group by feature/service

### Security
- Never commit .env files
- Use .env.example for documentation
- Validate required env vars at startup
- Use type-safe env vars (Zod schema)

## Common Patterns

### Async Component (Server)
```typescript
interface PageProps {
  params: { id: string }
  searchParams: { [key: string]: string | string[] | undefined }
}

export default async function Page({ params, searchParams }: PageProps) {
  const data = await fetchData(params.id)
  return <div>{/* render */}</div>
}
```

### Client Component with Hooks
```typescript
'use client'

import { useState } from 'react'

interface ComponentProps {
  initialValue: string
}

export function Component({ initialValue }: ComponentProps) {
  const [value, setValue] = useState(initialValue)
  return <div>{/* render */}</div>
}
```

### Form with Validation
```typescript
'use client'

import { useForm } from 'react-hook-form'
import { zodResolver } from '@hookform/resolvers/zod'
import * as z from 'zod'

const schema = z.object({
  email: z.string().email(),
  name: z.string().min(2)
})

type FormData = z.infer<typeof schema>

export function MyForm() {
  const { register, handleSubmit, formState: { errors } } = useForm<FormData>({
    resolver: zodResolver(schema)
  })

  const onSubmit = (data: FormData) => {
    // handle submission
  }

  return <form onSubmit={handleSubmit(onSubmit)}>{/* fields */}</form>
}
```

## Final Notes

- Prioritize readability over cleverness
- Optimize for maintainability
- Follow the principle of least surprise
- Write code that your future self will thank you for
- When in doubt, keep it simple
